01:44.220 --> 01:44.380
Right.

01:44.380 --> 01:45.580
We don't have this effect.

01:45.580 --> 01:47.700
We have just one average for each window.

01:47.700 --> 01:51.900
So now in order to have the effect of the moving average, it's going to be like the running total.

01:52.060 --> 01:55.740
We have to use the aggregate function together with the order by.

01:55.980 --> 01:59.380
So I'm just going to make it a new column.

01:59.500 --> 02:01.860
I'm just going to copy everything like here.

02:02.320 --> 02:02.880
and now.

02:02.920 --> 02:03.840
What are we going to do?

02:03.880 --> 02:04.680
Order by.

02:05.520 --> 02:09.480
And since it's over the time, we're going to go and use the order dates.

02:09.920 --> 02:11.360
Order dates.

02:11.360 --> 02:14.280
And we're going to have it as ascending because it's over time.

02:14.280 --> 02:14.760
Over time.

02:14.760 --> 02:18.720
Always like start with the earliest dates end up with the latest dates.

02:18.880 --> 02:21.960
So from the lowest to the highest we're going to leave it like this.

02:22.080 --> 02:25.200
So let's call it moving average.

02:25.240 --> 02:27.040
So now let's go and execute it.

02:27.080 --> 02:30.200
And we got here an extra comma because of the copy paste.

02:30.320 --> 02:31.720
So let's execute it again.

02:31.760 --> 02:32.120
All right.

02:32.120 --> 02:33.680
So now let's check the results.

02:33.720 --> 02:35.680
Let's take the first window over here.

02:35.880 --> 02:39.320
And you can see we have on the moving average like a progress.

02:39.480 --> 02:42.760
So it starts with 1015 1435.

02:43.000 --> 02:44.840
So there is like moving average.

02:44.840 --> 02:47.880
We don't have one solid number for the average.

02:48.000 --> 02:49.400
We have different values.

02:49.440 --> 02:51.160
So now how is SQL going to solve this.

02:51.200 --> 02:52.040
It's really simple.

02:52.080 --> 02:53.520
It's going to start row by row.

02:53.560 --> 02:55.880
So the first row what is the average of ten.

02:55.920 --> 02:56.880
It's going to be ten.

02:57.080 --> 03:00.920
Then moving on to the next one it's going to be ten plus 20 divided by two.

03:00.960 --> 03:02.200
You will get 15.

03:02.400 --> 03:04.490
So now moving to the third one.

03:04.530 --> 03:08.530
Although three values can be summarized divided by three, you will get 40.

03:08.730 --> 03:11.170
And now to the last row in the window.

03:11.570 --> 03:15.130
It's going to be summarizing all those four values divided by four.

03:15.170 --> 03:16.610
And you will get 35.

03:16.810 --> 03:21.890
And this is exactly the same value in the previous column you have here the average by products we don't

03:21.890 --> 03:26.290
have order by you got as well 35 exactly like the last row.

03:26.410 --> 03:28.530
And that's because we have the same calculation.

03:28.570 --> 03:32.050
It is summarizing all those four values dividing it by four.

03:32.090 --> 03:34.370
But now it's interesting the next value.

03:34.370 --> 03:37.890
So as you can see the next value it comes from another window.

03:38.170 --> 03:41.450
So you see here we have 15 for the product 102.

03:41.850 --> 03:44.010
But the average is going to be as well 15.

03:44.010 --> 03:48.290
So SQL is not considering the old values from the other window.

03:48.530 --> 03:51.490
So SQL is going to calculate each window separately.

03:51.490 --> 03:52.650
So it's again here.

03:52.650 --> 03:56.490
This is the first value of this window 15 the average 15.

03:56.530 --> 03:58.130
Then the same stuff right.

03:58.170 --> 04:00.810
So summarizing those values divided by two and so on.

04:00.810 --> 04:03.150
And this recall in data analysis Analyses.

04:03.350 --> 04:06.230
This last field over here, we call it a moving average.

04:06.230 --> 04:11.630
And you can implement it very simply using an average function together with the order by.

04:11.670 --> 04:12.070
All right.

04:12.070 --> 04:13.110
Let's move to the next task.

04:13.110 --> 04:19.990
And it says calculate the moving average of sales for each product over time including only the next

04:19.990 --> 04:20.550
order.

04:20.750 --> 04:23.430
So as you can see, the first part we have already done is right.

04:23.430 --> 04:27.350
We have the moving average and divided by partition by for the products.

04:27.350 --> 04:32.190
But here we have more specifications that says including only the next order.

04:32.230 --> 04:37.150
That means we are talking about the current order and as well the next order.

04:37.230 --> 04:40.310
So here we have like a fixed frame or fixed window.

04:40.310 --> 04:42.910
So we don't need the whole average of the window.

04:43.070 --> 04:46.830
We need only maximum two orders in each calculation.

04:46.830 --> 04:47.830
So how are we going to do that.

04:47.830 --> 04:52.110
We can have our custom frame clause inside our window function.

04:52.110 --> 04:54.830
So that means we cannot leave it as a default.

04:54.830 --> 04:56.790
We have to specify it.

04:56.790 --> 04:58.150
So let's go and do that.

04:58.190 --> 05:02.390
I will just copy the old definition of the window because we have the exact stuff.

05:02.670 --> 05:07.570
So we have the average sales over partition by product ID order by date.

05:07.570 --> 05:09.850
So this is the first part.

05:09.890 --> 05:12.570
So now we would like to have this fixed window.

05:12.570 --> 05:15.170
So we're going to go now and define our frame close.

05:15.210 --> 05:17.170
I'm just gonna zoom out a little bit.

05:17.290 --> 05:19.130
It's going to be rows between.

05:19.370 --> 05:23.330
So we have now the boundaries of the frame it says including the next order.

05:23.330 --> 05:25.410
So we're going to go and use the following.

05:25.450 --> 05:28.130
So the first boundary is going to be the current row.

05:28.130 --> 05:32.090
And since it's next order so it's going to be one following.

05:32.370 --> 05:35.770
So that is our frame including only the next order.

05:35.930 --> 05:41.770
And we have it like this one following let's call it yeah rolling average.

05:41.770 --> 05:42.570
So that's it.

05:42.570 --> 05:43.930
Let's go and execute.

05:43.930 --> 05:45.410
So now let's go and check the result.

05:45.410 --> 05:50.130
You can see the moving average has completely different values as the rolling average.

05:50.130 --> 05:52.850
So let's go and understand why we're going to do it row by row.

05:52.890 --> 05:54.610
Let's take the first row over here.

05:54.610 --> 05:58.690
So the sales here is ten and the rolling average is 15.

05:58.690 --> 06:00.010
So why is that.

06:00.010 --> 06:03.490
Because in the calculation we are considering the next value.

06:03.690 --> 06:07.910
So ten plus 20 divided by two you will get 15.

06:07.910 --> 06:10.950
So that means the SQL define the frame like this.

06:10.990 --> 06:14.030
Those two rows for this calculation for the first row.

06:14.030 --> 06:16.070
So now moving on to the second row.

06:16.190 --> 06:20.470
SQL can include as well the third one right the next one.

06:20.590 --> 06:25.870
But since the window is only two orders it's going to go and drop the first row.

06:26.070 --> 06:28.590
So the next frame is going to be like this.

06:28.870 --> 06:32.510
And as you can see it's going to be 20 plus 19 divided by two.

06:32.550 --> 06:33.830
You will get 55.

06:33.870 --> 06:37.150
So now you can see the effect of the rolling average right.

06:37.310 --> 06:39.310
So now for the next one it's going to be exact same.

06:39.310 --> 06:40.870
So we are at the third row.

06:40.910 --> 06:43.070
It's going to go and include the next one.

06:43.310 --> 06:44.590
And we're going to get the same value.

06:44.590 --> 06:48.030
Because 19 plus 20 divided by two you will get 55.

06:48.070 --> 06:51.150
Now interesting to the last row in the window over here.

06:51.470 --> 06:56.350
It will not go and consider the next value because it is outside of the window.

06:56.350 --> 07:00.150
So it's going to be 20 and it's going to stay as will 20.

